/**

@page exahype_performance_optimisation Performance Optimisation in ExaHyPE


This section discusses some ExaHyPE-specific performance optimisation steps.
While ExaHyPE and its baseline, Peano, provide simplistic performance analysis 
features to spot some flaws, a proper performance optimisation would likely
rely on external performance analysis tools.
Please read @ref peano_performance_optimisation "Peano's generic performance optimisation remarks" parallel to this 
document.
All of the recommendations on  apply for ExaHyPE, too.



## Single core optimisation

### The code is extremely bandwidth-bound and seems to move data forth and back all the time

Most ExaHyPE solvers store their data on (call) stacks to localise all memory 
accesses. This implies that they move their data around a lot. You can alter 
this behaviour by switching to a heap-based memory administration. Consult the 
documentation of the solver of interest, but the ``fix'' always looks similar to 

         self._patch.generator = peano4.datamodel.PatchToDoubleArrayOnHeap(self._patch,"double")

i.e. you have to swap how a patch is mapped onto a C++ implementation. Most solvers provide a routine

         my_solver.switch_to_heap_storage(True, True)


which does the switch for you. Please around with different True, False 
combinations. See also next section on AVX/alignment.



## Multithreading optimisation

A quick first insight into the single node behaviour can be obtained through Peano's
@ref peano_performance_optimisation "scaling analysis scripts".
Consult the @ref peano_runtime_analysis "Peano runtime analysis" remarks as well.



## MPI optimisation




\begin{itemize}
  \item {\bf I do not profit from more cores}. There can be many reasons for
  this, but I recommend to check a few things first:
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item Is your problem big enough per rank or are you maybe using too many
    nodes/MPI ranks? If so, you should either accept that your problem is simply
    too small for this number of nodes or jump down to next item. Read through
    the other suggestions first, though.
    \item Does the code spawn enough subtrees per node? If not, you might artificially restrict the number of trees
    per node in your load balancing, i.e.~set a maximum number of trees.
    Increase the number of trees per rank!
    \item Have you already switched from a naive solver to one with enclave
    tasking? Almost all of \ExaHyPE's solvers are offered as version with
    enclave tasking which means that they employ domain decomposition plus
    tasking on top.
    \begin{remark}
Enclave tasking often makes your code {\bf slower} initially. Ensure you use the
heap storage (see remarks above) and then consider to switch the compute kernels
(see remarks below).
    \end{remark}
  \end{enumerate}
  \item {\bf Enclave tasking is very slow}. This is a meta-question, i.e.~you
    have to run through a series of checks:
    \begin{enumerate}
      \item Switch to the heap data structure (see remark above).
      \item 
    \end{enumerate} 
  \item {\bf I cannot use more trees per rank, and enclave tasking doesn't make
    my code faster either}. This phenomenon often goes hand in hand with a lack
    of enclave tasks: The problem is simply too small to find enough tasks.
    There are a few tasks, but as tasks can only run on one single core, the
    code struggles to keep all cores busy.
    Check if you can work with more regular grids. They usually help you
      to generate more tasks. So avoid steep AMR changes.
  \item {\bf I use enclave tasking and have more trees per node than
  cores (or can't get more trees), but still the code does not use all
  the compute resources}. Switch the compute kernel.
  For the FD4 scheme and OpenMP, we provide for example compute kernels which theoretically can spread out over the
      whole node. The kernel is slower than the default version on one core, but
      it can use further cores and thus eventually becomes faster. Usually, you
      have to follow a few steps to enable this alternative kernel:
      \begin{enumerate}
        \item Switch to an enclave solver.
        \item Instruct the enclave solver that there it can use stateless PDE
        terms. For this, pass the constructor the argument
        \texttt{pde\_terms\_without\_state=True}.
        \item Ensure that the routine \texttt{patchCanUseStatelessPDETerms}
        return true most of the time (this is the default).
        \item Make the solver use a solver on the CPU which uses the batched
        taskgraph realisation.
        Most \ExaHyPE\ solver have an attribute similar to \newline
        \texttt{self.\_fused\_compute\_kernel\_call\_cpu} which is initialised
        with some default kernel call. Browse through your code and alter it
        such that it uses
\begin{code}
solver_variant                           = SolverVariant.Multicore,
kernel_variant                           = KernelVariant.BatchedAoSHeap,
\end{code}
        and a batched solver variant (patch-wise or similar makes limited
        sense). Usually, the kernels are constructed in \texttt{set\_implementation}, so 
        it might make sense to plug into this one (overwrite it, call the 
        supertype's implementation, and then switch the kernel).
    \end{enumerate} 
  \item {\bf The multithreading is very imbalanced, i.e.~I see a lot of trees
  per rank, but this does not materialise in higher speed}.
  The phenomenon typically goes hand in hand with high Spin Times (in VTune,
  e.g.). Operations like \texttt{\_\_kmp\_fork\_barrier} appear high up in the list of the most expensive routine. There are different things to check:
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item Does the code yield subtrees which are extremely ill-balanced? In this
    case, you might might want to reduce the number of ranks per tree. \ExaHyPE\
    reports how big the trees are via
    \begin{bash}
rank:1  toolbox::loadbalancing::dumpStatistics()    info    2 tree(s): \
  (#1:13/716)(#3:14/715) total=27/1431 (local/remote)
    \end{bash}
    In this case, we have only two trees and they are of almost the
    same size (13 vs.~14). But if these numbers differ dramatically,
    you might want to reduce the number of trees.
    \item Does the code use enclave tasks but does not yield many tasks? In this
    case, try a more regular mesh, i.e.~artificially refine to avoid steep
    resolution changes.
    \item Does the code use enclave tasks and yield many tasks? In this case,
    you might have encountered a scheduler flaw. Change the multicore scheduler
    on the command line.
  \end{enumerate}
  \item {\bf The code seems to fail to use aligned data}. See general remarks
  in documentation of \texttt{tarch::allocateMemory()} and
  \texttt{tarch::MemoryLocation}.
  \item {\bf The PDE term routines (sources, ncp, fluxes, eigenvalues,
  \ldots) are extremely expensive}. In principle, that's what you want to see.
  If this is the case, then you should next follow the recommendations for GPU
  parallelisation (cmp.~\ref{section:exahype:gpu-support}). These focus on GPU
  efficiency, but the optimisations also pay off significantly for CPUs.
\end{itemize}


 */
 